 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed revisions reach browsers through a self-hosted WebSocket service that manages sessions and publishes updates, backed by a replayable change store and browser reconnect/catch-up. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were considered. Polling would have kept delivery stateless but adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag; a hosted service would have cut connection operations but introduces recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosting keeps the board independent of a realtime vendor and delivers fast updates, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect behavior. The options, criteria, and tradeoff are recorded in [the change-delivery decision](decisions/change-delivery-approach.md).
